  'Daily fluid requirements depend directly on metabolic body mass, respiratory evaporative loss, sweat rates during physical exercise, and ambient climate temperature. The European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) and U.S. National Academies recommend a baseline fluid intake of 30–35 mL per kg of body weight, plus 350–500 mL of additional hydration for every 30 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous exercise.', '{"title":"EFSA & National Academies Daily Hydration Guidelines","badge":"Clinical Hydration Standard","stat":"35 mL / kg Body Weight + 500 mL / 30 min Exercise","description":"Baseline: Men ≈ 2.5–3.0 L/day, Women ≈ 2.0–2.2 L/day from all fluid and beverage sources. High-temperature environments and elevated sweat rates require isotonic electrolyte balance to avoid hyponatremia.","source":"European Food Safety Authority (EFSA Journal) & U.S. National Academies of Sciences","lastUpdated":"January 2026"}',
  '[{"step":1,"title":"Enter Your Body Weight","description":"Provide your current body weight in kilograms.","icon":"weight"},{"step":2,"title":"Select Activity Level","description":"Choose your daily physical activity level.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":3,"title":"Calculate Water Intake","description":"The calculator estimates your recommended daily hydration needs.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":4,"title":"View Results","description":"See your daily water intake recommendation in liters and glasses.","icon":"result"}]', '{"title":"Water Intake Formula","formula":"Daily Water Intake (Liters) = Body Weight (kg) × 0.033","explanation":"A commonly used hydration guideline recommends approximately 33 milliliters of water per kilogram of body weight each day. Additional water may be required for exercise, hot weather, or specific health conditions.","example":{"input":"Weight: 70 kg","output":"Recommended Water Intake: 2.31 Liters per Day"},"useCases":["Daily Hydration Planning","Fitness Programs","Weight Loss Plans","Healthy Lifestyle Management","Athletic Performance","Nutrition Planning","Health Monitoring","Exercise Recovery","Wellness Tracking","Hydration Goals"]}', '[{"question":"How much water should I drink per day?","answer":"Most healthy adults need somewhere between 2.7 and 3.7 liters of total fluid a day, with women generally sitting toward the lower end of that range and men toward the higher end. This total includes water from beverages as well as the moisture naturally found in food. Your own number can shift based on body size, how active you are, the climate you live in, and your overall health, which is exactly why a calculator that factors in your weight and activity level gives a far more useful estimate than a single flat number everyone is told to follow."},{"question":"Does exercise affect water requirements?","answer":"Yes, and often more significantly than people expect. Sweating during exercise causes your body to lose both water and electrolytes, and the harder or longer you train, the greater that loss becomes. As a general guideline, drinking a small amount before a workout, sipping regularly during activity, and rehydrating afterward based on how much you sweat will keep you performing at your best. Endurance athletes in particular should pay attention to replacing electrolytes alongside plain water, since sweat carries away more than just fluid."},{"question":"Can I drink too much water?","answer":"It is possible, though genuinely uncommon in everyday life. Drinking an extremely large volume of water in a very short window can dilute the sodium in your blood, a condition known as hyponatremia. Mild cases can cause headache, nausea, or confusion, while severe cases are medical emergencies. This mostly affects people who consume excessive amounts of water in a short period, such as during endurance events, rather than someone who drinks water steadily and moderately throughout an ordinary day."},{"question":"Do coffee and tea count toward water intake?","answer":"Yes. Despite the old myth that caffeinated drinks dehydrate you, research shows that coffee, tea, and other caffeinated beverages still contribute positively to your daily fluid total for most people who consume them in normal amounts. Plain water remains the best primary source because it has no added sugar or calories, but you do not need to mentally subtract your coffee or tea from your hydration goal."},{"question":"Why is hydration important?","answer":"Water is involved in almost every function your body performs. It helps regulate temperature, cushions joints, carries nutrients and oxygen to cells, supports digestion, and allows your kidneys to filter waste efficiently. Even a small drop in hydration can affect concentration, mood, and physical performance, which is why maintaining a consistent intake throughout the day matters more than trying to catch up all at once in the evening."},{"question":"How do electrolytes affect hydration compared to drinking plain water?","answer":"Electrolytes like sodium and potassium maintain fluid balance across cellular walls. When exercising or sweating heavily, consuming electrolytes alongside water helps your body retain intracellular fluid and prevents sodium dilution in the bloodstream."},{"question":"How does body weight affect how much water I need?","answer":"Larger bodies generally contain more total water and support more metabolic activity, so they typically require a higher fluid intake to stay properly hydrated. This is why weight-based formulas, like multiplying your body weight in kilograms by roughly 0.033, tend to produce a more personalized estimate than a one-size-fits-all number such as eight glasses a day, which was never based on individual differences in body size to begin with."},{"question":"Do pregnant or breastfeeding women need more water?","answer":"Generally, yes. Pregnancy increases blood volume and supports the growing needs of the fetus, while breastfeeding uses a substantial amount of fluid to produce milk. Many health guidelines suggest that pregnant women add a modest amount to their baseline intake and that breastfeeding women increase their intake further still. Because individual needs vary, it is a good idea to check with a healthcare provider for personalized guidance during these stages."},{"question":"Does hot weather change my daily water needs?","answer":"It does. Higher temperatures and humidity increase sweat loss even when you are not exercising, so your body needs extra fluid simply to maintain its normal temperature and function. People living in hot climates, working outdoors, or traveling somewhere warmer than they are used to should expect their hydration target to rise, sometimes by a liter or more depending on conditions."},{"question":"What are the early signs that I''m not drinking enough water?","answer":"Common early indicators include a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, fatigue, mild headache, and difficulty concentrating. Thirst itself is actually a fairly late signal, meaning that by the time you feel thirsty, mild dehydration may have already begun. Paying attention to urine color throughout the day is one of the simplest and most reliable ways to gauge your hydration status at a glance."},{"question":"Should children drink the same amount of water as adults?","answer":"No. Children generally need less total fluid than adults because of their smaller body size, though their needs still increase with age, activity level, and warm weather. Rather than applying an adult formula directly, it is best to follow age-appropriate guidance from a pediatrician, since children''s fluid needs are calculated differently than those of adults."},{"question":"Does age affect how much water I should drink?","answer":"Yes, to some degree. Older adults often experience a reduced sense of thirst, which can make it easier to become mildly dehydrated without noticing. At the same time, certain medications and health conditions that become more common with age can also influence fluid needs. This makes it especially important for older adults to drink water consistently throughout the day rather than relying solely on feeling thirsty as a reminder."},{"question":"Is bottled water better than tap water for hydration?","answer":"From a hydration standpoint, both are equally effective, since your body absorbs water the same way regardless of its source. The choice between bottled and tap water usually comes down to taste preference, local water quality, convenience, and environmental considerations rather than any meaningful difference in how well each one hydrates you."}]',
