  NULL, NULL,
  '[{"step":1,"title":"Select Your State or UT","description":"Choose the state or union territory where you plan to register your vehicle, since road tax is a state subject and rates vary significantly across India.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":2,"title":"Choose Vehicle Type & Fuel","description":"Select whether you''re registering a car, bike, or commercial vehicle, and specify the fuel type — petrol, diesel, CNG, or electric — since tax slabs differ by fuel category in most states.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":3,"title":"Enter Ex-Showroom Price","description":"Input the exact ex-showroom price of your vehicle as mentioned on your invoice. In Gujarat, Chandigarh, and Jharkhand, this should be the pre-GST invoice value instead.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":4,"title":"View Road Tax & RTO Breakdown","description":"Get an instant breakdown of your applicable road tax percentage, total road tax amount, fixed registration charges, and the overall addition to your on-road price.","icon":"result"}]', '{"title":"Road Tax Calculator Formula","formula":"Road Tax = Ex-Showroom Price × Applicable State Tax Slab (%)","explanation":"Road tax, officially called Motor Vehicle Tax, is a one-time levy collected by state governments at the time of vehicle registration under the Motor Vehicles Act, 1988. Unlike GST, which is a central, uniform tax, road tax is a state subject — meaning every state and union territory sets its own percentage slabs based on vehicle price, fuel type, and sometimes engine capacity. For private vehicles, this tax is typically paid once and remains valid for 15 years as a lifetime tax (LTT), after which a smaller renewal fee applies for a further 5-year block. The tax is added on top of your ex-showroom price and insurance to arrive at the final on-road price you pay at the dealership.","example":{"input":"Ex-Showroom Price: ₹10,00,000 (Petrol Car), State: Delhi, Applicable Slab: 7%","output":"Road Tax = ₹10,00,000 × 7% = ₹70,000"},"useCases":["New car registration cost estimation","Bike and two-wheeler RTO tax calculation","Comparing road tax across different states before registration","Budgeting total on-road price of a vehicle","Checking EV road tax exemption eligibility","Understanding BH series registration tax structure","Commercial vehicle tax planning"]}', '[{"question":"How exactly is road tax calculated in India, and why does it vary so much between states?","answer":"Road tax in India is not governed by a single central formula — it falls under the State List of the Indian Constitution, which means every state legislature has full authority to set its own motor vehicle taxation rules under its respective state-level Motor Vehicles Taxation Act. In practice, this means the same car, at the same price, can attract a dramatically different road tax bill depending purely on where it gets registered. Most states use a slab-based percentage system, where the tax rate itself increases as the ex-showroom price of the vehicle rises. For instance, a budget hatchback priced under ₹6 lakh might attract a slab as low as 4%, while a premium SUV priced above ₹20 lakh in the same state could be taxed at 12% or higher. States like Karnataka and Kerala are known for particularly steep slabs on premium and luxury vehicles, sometimes touching 18-20%, while smaller states and union territories such as Dadra & Nagar Haveli, Sikkim, and Puducherry maintain comparatively lean rates to encourage vehicle registrations and dealership activity within their jurisdiction. A second major source of variation is the tax base itself. Most states calculate road tax on the full ex-showroom price of the vehicle, but a handful of states — notably Gujarat, Chandigarh, and Jharkhand — calculate it on the pre-GST invoice value instead, which is meaningfully lower since GST on cars ranges from 18% to 40% depending on vehicle size and category. This distinction alone can make an identical car''s registration cost thousands of rupees cheaper in one of these states compared to a state using the standard ex-showroom method. Fuel type adds another layer: diesel vehicles typically face a surcharge of 1% to 2% over petrol variants in most states due to their higher emission profile, while CNG vehicles sometimes receive a small concession. Finally, engine capacity and seating configuration can factor in for two-wheelers and commercial vehicles respectively, adding further granularity to what looks like a simple percentage on the surface but is actually a fairly layered calculation once you account for all the variables involved."},{"question":"Are electric vehicles exempt from road tax in India, and which states offer this benefit?","answer":"Yes, a significant number of Indian states currently offer either a full or partial road tax exemption on electric vehicles as part of their broader push toward green mobility and reduced vehicular emissions. As of the most recent state transport department notifications, around fourteen states and union territories — including Delhi, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, Punjab, Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Chandigarh, Jammu & Kashmir, and Ladakh — provide a complete 100% waiver on road tax for battery electric vehicles, meaning an EV buyer in these regions pays zero road tax regardless of the vehicle''s ex-showroom price. This exemption is usually available for both electric cars and electric two-wheelers, though some states cap the benefit to vehicles below a certain price threshold to prevent misuse for ultra-premium EVs. A second group of states, including Karnataka, Kerala, and Haryana, have moved toward a tiered EV taxation model rather than a blanket exemption — meaning EVs still attract some road tax, but at a substantially reduced percentage compared to petrol or diesel vehicles of similar value, often in the 1% to 4% range instead of the standard 8% to 15% bracket. Gujarat takes a slightly different approach, applying a nominal flat rate of around 1% specifically for electric vehicles rather than a full waiver. It''s worth noting that these policies are among the more frequently revised areas of state transport regulation, since they''re tied to evolving central government schemes like the FAME scheme and individual state EV policies, both of which get updated periodically as adoption targets and budget allocations shift. Buyers should always cross-check the latest notification from their specific state transport department or RTO before finalizing a purchase decision, since a state that offered a full exemption a year ago may have since moved to a tiered structure, or vice versa, as state governments continue to calibrate these incentives against their revenue needs and EV adoption goals."},{"question":"What are the fixed RTO charges I need to pay in addition to road tax when registering a new vehicle?","answer":"Beyond the percentage-based road tax itself, every vehicle registration in India involves a set of standardized fixed charges that apply uniformly regardless of which state you''re registering in, though minor variations of a few hundred rupees can occur. The first and most basic is the registration fee, which is typically ₹600 for private vehicles across most states, covering the administrative cost of the RTO officially recording your vehicle details and issuing your registration certificate. Next is the High Security Registration Plate (HSRP) charge, which usually ranges between ₹230 and ₹400 depending on your vehicle category and is now mandatory for all new vehicles as part of India''s standardized number plate security initiative. If you''re opting for FASTag, which is compulsory for most vehicles today to enable seamless toll payments, you''ll pay an additional ₹500 to ₹600, a portion of which is a refundable security deposit. For buyers financing their vehicle through a bank or NBFC loan, there''s also a hypothecation charge of approximately ₹1,500, which covers the RTO''s process of noting the financier''s lien on your registration certificate until the loan is fully repaid — this charge simply doesn''t apply if you''re paying the full amount upfront in cash. Some states also levy smaller miscellaneous charges for smart card issuance or temporary registration extensions if there''s a delay between purchase and permanent registration. Altogether, these fixed charges typically add up to somewhere between ₹2,500 and ₹4,000 on top of your road tax amount, and while they''re a relatively small fraction of your total on-road price compared to the road tax slab itself, they''re worth budgeting for precisely because they''re often glossed over in initial price quotes from dealerships, only to appear as a lump sum during the final billing stage at delivery."},{"question":"What is BH series (Bharat series) registration, and does it reduce my road tax liability?","answer":"Bharat Series, commonly abbreviated as BH series registration, is a special vehicle registration scheme introduced by the Ministry of Road Transport and Highways specifically to simplify life for individuals who relocate frequently between states due to their profession, such as central and state government employees, defence personnel, and employees of private companies with offices in four or more states or union territories. Under the standard registration system, if you move to a new state for more than a defined period, you''re legally required to get your vehicle re-registered in that state and pay fresh road tax there, even though you already paid tax when you first registered the vehicle — a process that''s both expensive and administratively tedious for frequent movers. BH series registration solves this by allowing the vehicle to carry a uniform, portable registration mark that remains valid across state lines without requiring re-registration every time the owner relocates. In terms of tax structure, BH series vehicles pay their road tax in a different format compared to standard state registration — instead of a single lump-sum lifetime tax, the tax is typically paid in installments, either as a smaller amount every two years or as one lump sum calculated using a uniform, moderate percentage slab (commonly in the 8% to 10% range depending on vehicle price, with lower rates for electric vehicles) rather than being subject to whatever the highest or lowest neighboring state happens to charge. This effectively insulates the owner from having to pay full fresh road tax every time they cross a state border for work. Eligibility, however, is strictly limited to the categories mentioned above — an ordinary private buyer without a qualifying transferable job or government affiliation cannot opt for BH series purely to access a lower or more convenient tax structure, and attempting to misrepresent eligibility can result in registration cancellation and penalties from the regional transport authority."},{"question":"Can I save money by registering my vehicle in a state with lower road tax than where I actually live?","answer":"This is one of the most common questions vehicle buyers ask, and the honest answer is that doing so is not legally permitted and carries real financial and legal risk. Under Section 47 of the Motor Vehicles Act, a vehicle must be registered in the state where the owner ordinarily resides, meaning you''re required to provide valid address proof of that state at the time of registration — you simply cannot walk into an RTO in a low-tax state like Sikkim or Dadra & Nagar Haveli and register a vehicle there if you don''t actually live within that jurisdiction. Some buyers have historically attempted workarounds, such as registering through a relative''s address in a lower-tax state, but this creates a legally vulnerable situation: if your vehicle is later found operating predominantly in a different state from where it''s registered — which traffic police and RTO enforcement teams do check for, particularly for higher-value vehicles — you can be required to complete a fresh re-registration in your actual home state, effectively paying road tax twice, in addition to facing potential fines for the violation itself. There have been documented cases, including with high-end luxury vehicles, where such vehicles were impounded specifically because they carried registration marks from a state where the owner did not actually reside. If your genuine concern is minimizing repeated tax burdens due to legitimate cross-state relocation for work, the correct and fully legal route is the BH series registration scheme described above, which is specifically designed to solve this exact problem for eligible individuals without requiring any misrepresentation of residency. For everyone else, the only legitimate way to benefit from state-wise road tax differences is if you are genuinely and permanently relocating your residence to a lower-tax state, in which case registering there after establishing valid residency is entirely legal and simply a natural consequence of that state''s tax policy."},{"question":"Does road tax apply to the full ex-showroom price even if I received a discount or exchange bonus from the dealership?","answer":"Yes, and this catches a surprising number of buyers off guard during final billing. Road tax in nearly every Indian state is calculated strictly against the manufacturer''s declared ex-showroom price (or the pre-GST invoice value in the handful of states that use that method), and this calculation is entirely independent of whatever discount, cashback, exchange bonus, or promotional offer you may have negotiated with your dealership. In other words, if a car has an official ex-showroom price of ₹12 lakh but you managed to negotiate it down to an effective ₹11 lakh after a dealer discount and an old-car exchange bonus, your road tax will still be calculated on the full ₹12 lakh figure, not the discounted amount you actually paid. This is because road tax is a statutory levy tied to the vehicle''s declared manufacturing and invoicing value as recorded with the manufacturer, not the private commercial negotiation between you and the dealer, which the RTO has no visibility into or jurisdiction over. The only exception to this rule involves the handful of states that calculate tax on the pre-GST invoice value rather than the full ex-showroom price — in those cases, the tax base is still a fixed, declared figure rather than your negotiated purchase price, so the same principle applies just against a different baseline number. This is an important distinction to keep in mind when budgeting for a car purchase: many buyers mentally calculate their expected road tax based on the discounted price they''re paying, only to find the actual RTO bill is higher because it''s pegged to the undiscounted ex-showroom figure. When comparing total on-road costs across dealerships or negotiating a deal, it''s worth asking your dealer to clearly break down the ex-showroom price used for road tax calculation separately from any discounts applied to the vehicle''s selling price, so there are no surprises at the final registration and delivery stage."}]',
